  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 211 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nSeveral near-to-slightly-cooler-than-normal days await us for the \nupcoming weekend. Though the ridge will build back in over the \nSouthern Rockies, enough weakness in the ridge will exist for us to \nfeel the full extent of a prolonged period of northwest flow over \nthe Central Plains. That will keep northerly surface winds perhaps \nas long as Monday evening in our area. Lingering rain chances are \nexpected mostly southeast of I-44 on Saturday, but the rest of the \nweekend and into Monday looks much drier. \n\nThe one constant in 2026 has been that any cooldown can't last long, \nand sure enough, guidance and our gridded forecast are starting to \nwarm back up beginning next Tuesday. Fortunately, it looks like \nwe'll be doing \"October warm\" instead of \"July warm\", with \ntemperatures rebounding into the 80s at most sites. For those in \nsearch of more good news, model trends beyond the official long-term \nperiod do suggest that we'll be seeing more frontal intrusions in \nthe first half of October.\n\nMeister",
